  </section>
  <Feature
    id="unit-health"
    title="Units have health"
    caption="Health bars over the units, all over the world map."
    image="unit-health.png"
    secondCaption="Close-up: damaged units with their hit points and health bars next to Thebes."
    secondImage="unit-health-zoom.png"
  >
    <p>
      A standard unit has 3 hit points, a veteran 4, and an elite unit 5. Damage stays after the battle, and a health bar
      shows the current state on the map and during battles.
    </p>
    <p>
      Battles are fought in rounds: each round removes one hit point from the side that lost it. The original decided
      a battle with a single roll, so a Trireme could sink a Battleship; now a weak unit has to win several rounds in a row.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hit points are saved in a small file next to the saved game (<code>CIVIL0.HP</code> next to <code>CIVIL0.SVE</code>),
      so the original DOS game can still load the save without issues.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="load-game"
    title="Load Game in the game menu"
    caption="Load Game in the GAME menu."
    image="load-game.png"
  >
    <p>
      The in-game menu has a <strong>Load Game</strong> option, so you no longer have to exit to the main menu to load
      a save. The list shows your latest autosaves or manual saved games.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="saves"
    title="20 save slots and 20 autosaves"
    caption="20 autosaves, one for every turn."
    image="saves.png"
    secondCaption="20 slots for your own saved games."
    secondImage="saved-games.png"
  >
    <p>
      Save in 20 slots (<code>CIVIL0</code> to <code>CIVIL19</code>) instead of just 4. In addition, the game saves every turn
      to 20 rotating autosaves (<code>AUTO00</code> to <code>AUTO19</code>), overwriting the oldest, so you can go back up to
      20 turns. The original only had 4 autosaves, once every 1,000 years.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="city-projects"
    title="Continuous city projects & enhanced production menu"
    caption="Delhi production selection showing Wealth, Research, Food, turn estimates, and combat stats (ADM)."
    image="city-production-projects.png"
  >
    <p>
      Instead of producing units or buildings, cities can directly convert net shield production into gold, science, or food supplies:
    </p>
    <ul class="plain-list">
      <li><strong>Wealth:</strong> converts 50% of net shields directly into gold and taxes (requires Banking).</li>
      <li><strong>Research:</strong> converts 50% of net shields directly into science points (requires University).</li>
      <li><strong>Food:</strong> converts 50% of net shields directly into city food supplies (requires Engineering).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The build dialog now displays the precise number of turns to completion and unit combat statistics (ADM: Attack / Defense / Movement).
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="city-resources"
    title="Centered numeric resource badges in city view"
    caption="Close-up of Coptos resources: centered numeric badges and yield split for food (22/4), production (2/14), and trade."
    image="city-resources-badges.png"
  >
    <p>
      City resource rows now feature clear, readable numbers centered directly on wheat, shield, and coin icons.
    </p>
    <p>
      Food and production rows clearly display the split between consumed upkeep and surplus (e.g. <code>22/4</code> indicates 22 food consumed and 4 stored).
      The trade row breaks down total revenue into gold and science outputs (e.g. <code>27</code> gold coins and <code>19</code> science beakers).
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="railroads"
    title="Railroads through cities and transit networks"
    caption="Railroads run straight through the city (framed in red): trains cross it without losing movement points."
    image="city-railroads-network.png"
  >
    <p>
      A city receives railroads the moment its owner discovers Railroad technology. In the original game a city only
      had a road, so a train lost a third of its moves in every city it passed through.
    </p>
    <p>
      Movement along rail tracks through cities is now completely free of movement cost, also supporting GoTo orders and AI players.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="civilization-notes"
    title="Tactical advice & notes with Alt+H"
    caption="Civilization Note: tactical advisor guidelines accessible at any time via the Alt+H keyboard shortcut."
    image="civilization-notes-help.png"
  >
    <p>
      Pressing <strong>Alt+H</strong> displays current strategic notes and helpful advisor insights (Civilization Notes)
      at any turn and any year during your campaign.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the original 1991 release, help was locked after 0 AD and required the global help option enabled. Now tactical guidance
      remains available throughout the ancient, medieval, and modern eras.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="sound-upgrade"
    title="8 audio modes & authentic DOS setup"
    caption="Authentic DOS hardware setup screen: select graphics, sound (8 modes), and controls."
    image="sound-mode-select.png"
  >
    <p>
      The game starts with authentic 1991 setup screens rendered in 640×400 with IBM PC ROM BIOS fonts.
      Features 8 selectable sound modes: 1-bit PC Speaker, newly restored Tandy 1000 3-voice sound (TI SN76496),
      AdLib OPL2 FM, official MicroProse 486 (ASOUND2) & YM262 (ASOUND3) patches, Roland MT-32 translation,
      and native direct playback of 18 General MIDI (.MID) arrangements.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read all about the audio systems on the dedicated <a href={`${base}/sound/`}>Sound & Music page</a>.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="wonders"
    title="Wonders of the World never expire"
    caption="Memphis with the Hanging Gardens, still keeping citizens happy."
    image="wonders.png"
  >
    <p>
      In the original game most Wonders of the World stopped working when any player discovered a particular technology:
      the Hanging Gardens with Invention, the Pyramids with Communism, and the Great Library with University.
      Now a Wonder keeps working as long as you control the city it was built in.
    </p>
    <p>The Civilopedia describes the wonders without the expiration notes.</p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="upgrades"
    title="Upgrade units for gold"
    caption="Upgrade to Phalanx in the Orders menu for the Militia in Thebes."
    image="upgrades.png"
    secondCaption="The Civilopedia explains what a unit upgrades to and for how much."
    secondImage="upgrades-civilopedia.png"
  >
    <p>
      An obsolete unit in your city can be upgraded to its successor: <strong>Orders → Upgrade</strong> or the
      <code>E</code> key. The unit keeps its veteran or elite rank and its hit points, and uses up its move for the turn.
    </p>
    <ul class="plain-list">
      <li>Horsemen → Chariot → Knights → Armor</li>
      <li>Militia → Phalanx → Musketeers → Riflemen → Mech. Inf.</li>
      <li>Legion → Musketeers</li>
      <li>Catapult → Cannon → Artillery</li>
      <li>Trireme → Sail → Transport (civilian ships)</li>
      <li>Frigate → Ironclad → Cruiser → Battleship (warships)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The cost matches the rush-buy price of the missing shields in the city (at least $30). The computer opponents
      upgrade their units as well.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="demographics"
    title="Demographics"
    caption="The demographics of a large empire: approval and productivity counted right."
    image="demographics.png"
  >
    <p>
      The demographics screen used 16-bit numbers and wrapped around in large empires, showing negative approval,
      production, and disease numbers. The values are now calculated correctly.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="leader-portraits"
    title="Own leader portraits for Poland and Japan"
    caption="Casimir dresses for every audience scenery: seven outfits by government and era."
    image="leader-portraits.png"
    wide
  >
    <p>
      In an audience the Polish and Japanese leaders no longer borrow the faces of Gandhi and Caesar. <strong>Casimir the Great</strong> changes his dress with the scenery, which follows the government and the era: a warlord in helmet and furs or a field uniform under despotism, a crown and royal robe or a parade uniform under monarchy, a party tunic under communism, a senator's toga or a suit under a republic or democracy. <strong>Tokugawa</strong> has his own portrait. Both blink and speak like the original leaders. The computer plays Poland in every game you do not play it: in the Romans' slot, or in another one when you take the Romans, Russians or Japan.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="larger-map"
    title="Larger map window"
    caption="Map: 30 × 24 tiles — four times as much of the world on one screen."
    image="larger-map.png"
    wide
  >
    <p>
      <strong>View → Map</strong> shows more of the world at once: 15×12 tiles as in the original, or 20×15, 30×24 (four times as much map), 40×30 or 50×36 tiles. The tiles keep their size, so a bigger window simply shows more land instead of bigger pixels. The left panel stretches down, the minimap frame follows the window, and screens drawn for 320×200 (city, advisors, Civilopedia, messages) appear centred over the map. The choice is remembered.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="diplomacy-advisor"
    title="Diplomacy rework and War Advisor (F12)"
    caption="War Advisor (F12): treaties, army strength, mood and vendetta with the reparations it would cost."
    image="war-advisor.png"
    secondCaption="Details of the Mongols: vengeful mood and its reasons, the vendetta with its reparations, and a forecast of the next audience."
    secondImage="war-advisor-details.png"
  >
    <p>
      One sneak attack no longer means endless war. Vendettas expire (48 turns, longer for repeat offenders), expensive reparations buy forgiveness, treaties last at least 16 turns, a ruler drops plans to break the peace when you are much stronger, and long peace builds trust. The warning before breaking a treaty shows the vendetta and the reparations it will cost. The War Advisor shows peace length, mood and army for every civilization; press 1-6 for the reasons of hostility or friendship, a forecast of the next audience (tribute, peace offer, technology exchange, hidden betrayal) and advice, press <code>C</code> for every condition with its current value (green for us, red against us), and with an embassy press <code>T</code> to talk to that ruler at once. A long peace also eases the envy rulers feel for the world leader without nuclear weapons: after 32 turns a good mood outweighs it, after 60 it is gone.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="production-queue"
    title="Production queue"
    caption="The production queue (Q or QUE) in the city screen."
    image="production-queue.png"
  >
    <p>
      Press <code>Q</code> or the <code>QUE</code> button (between CHANGE and BUY, light green while the queue is not empty) in the city screen to plan up to five items; the city starts the next one by itself when the current item is finished, and items that became impossible are skipped. The production box of the city screen now spans the full width so all three buttons fit.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="automated-settlers"
    title="Automated settlers"
    caption="Order A: the five modes of automated settlers."
    image="automated-settlers.png"
  >
    <p>
      Order <code>A</code> (also <strong>Orders → Automate Settlers</strong>) with five modes: roads only, improve everything, improve without replacing existing work, clean up pollution, and connect cities with roads and later railroads. They work around all cities or only around the nearest one, show an <code>A</code> on their icon, and develop city land in a sensible order: grassland with a shield, plains, grassland without a shield — road first, then irrigation — while pollution is always cleaned first. A settler standing on grassland, plains, desert or a river (with Bridge Building) builds the quick road first. Jungle and swamp are drained into grassland, but jungle with gems becomes forest (game) and swamp with oil only gets a road. Two automated settlers never pick the same tile; selecting the unit cancels the automation.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="disorder-warning"
    title="Civil disorder warning"
    caption="Before the end of turn: the cities that would riot, with automatic entertainers."
    image="disorder-warning.png"
  >
    <p>
      When you end your turn, the game checks every city as it will be at the start of the next turn. If some would riot, it lists them and offers to <em>assign entertainers</em> (the least productive workers become entertainers until each city is calm), to end the turn anyway, or to go back to the map and fix them yourself.
    </p>
  </Feature>

  <Feature
    id="city-rules"
    title="Civilopedia entry City Rules"
    caption="Civilopedia → Miscellaneous → City Rules."
    image="civilopedia-city-rules.png"
  >
    <p>
      <strong>Civilopedia → Miscellaneous → City Rules</strong> explains corruption and the Courthouse, the civil disorder warning and the sentry alarm; the <em>War</em> entry describes the new Senate rule.
    </p>
  </Feature>

