  <h2 id="smaller-fixes">Smaller fixes and refinements</h2>
  <ul class="plain-list">
    <li id="multiplatform-native"><strong>Native Multiplatform (Windows, Linux, macOS):</strong> Runs 100% natively on 64-bit Windows, Linux, and macOS (native builds for both Apple Silicon M-series and Intel x64) without DOSBox, Wine, or any runtime installation.</li>
    <li id="new-civilizations"><strong>DLC — Poland & Japan:</strong> Full playable Polish and Japanese civilizations with custom striped regalia, authentic MIDI anthems, and historical Earth start locations (see <a href="#dlc-poland-japan">detailed DLC overview above</a>).</li>
    <li id="advisor-reports"><strong>Richer advisor reports:</strong> Research pace (+bulbs/turn) and remaining progress in the Science Report, total luxury, gross income, science pace (turns left), and net treasury cashflow in the Trade Report, plus column headers and empire-wide totals in the City Status Report.</li>
    <li id="elite-units"><strong>Elite units:</strong> A veteran that wins a battle becomes an elite unit and receives a fifth hit point. In every battle an elite unit may roll two lost combat rounds again. The unit panel shows <code>Elite</code> and unit lists mark it <code>(E)</code>.</li>
    <li id="goto-pathfinding"><strong>Smarter GoTo:</strong> Units take the fastest route over the whole map instead of the original short-range search (a 16×16 window and a coarse block graph that knew nothing about railroads): they stay on railroads, use roads, avoid enemy units and foreign cities, and do not zigzag.</li>
    <li id="stack-combat"><strong>Fairer stack combat:</strong> A stack beaten on land outside a city or fortress no longer dies entirely: the defender falls, the others lose 2 hit points and only the weakest die.</li>
    <li id="ai-expansion"><strong>Expanding computer opponents:</strong> The original computer left wide gaps between its cities (an average site needed 5-7 tiles to the next city), searched for sites by walking in random directions and rarely had more than one settler. Now its settlers head for the best free site nearby, keeping 5 tiles between cities (4 on a small island). From Prince up the computer builds settlers first until it has 10 (Prince), 16 (King) or 24 (Emperor) cities, adjusted by the leader's expansion trait, and when its land is full it carries settlers to free islands by Trireme, Sail or Transport. Chieftain and Warlord stay as mild as before. Measured in headless games on Emperor (every civilization computer controlled, 110-150 turns), the computer founds 21-60% more cities than in the original until the land is full.</li>
    <li id="sentry-alarm"><strong>Sentry alarm:</strong> In the original game a sentried unit woke up only when an enemy moved next to it. Now every sentried unit with a unit of a civilization you are not at peace with (or a barbarian) next to it wakes up at the start of your turn, also inside cities. Land units sleeping aboard a ship at sea keep sleeping.</li>
    <li id="courthouse"><strong>Stronger Courthouse:</strong> Corruption grows with the distance from the Palace, and the original Courthouse only halved it, so distant cities and colonies stayed poor. The Courthouse now removes three quarters of the corruption.</li>
    <li id="senate"><strong>A Senate that listens:</strong> In a Republic or Democracy the Senate still forbids sneak attacks, but it lets you break a treaty — and refuse a peace offer — when the other civilization holds a vendetta against you, or when your embassy has exposed its plan to break the peace.</li>
    <li id="healing"><strong>Unit healing:</strong> Units recover hit points every turn (2 HP in a friendly city, 1 HP fortified in the field, 1 HP for naval units resting near coastlines up to 3 tiles from a city; air units heal strictly in cities).</li>
    <li id="goto-railroads"><strong>Free GoTo railroad movement:</strong> Movement along rail lines is completely free of cost also when executing GoTo orders and for computer opponents.</li>
    <li id="pillage"><strong>Safe pillaging:</strong> Pillaging tiles directly adjacent to foreign cities no longer corrupts game memory or data structures.</li>
    <li id="random-numbers"><strong>Modern RNG algorithm:</strong> Uses the fast and proven xoshiro128** generator instead of the archaic 1991 pseudorandom sequence.</li>
    <li id="naval-wonders"><strong>Enhanced naval wonders:</strong> The Lighthouse and Magellan's Expedition increase ship movement by +1 point each (+2 points with both wonders active).</li>
    <li id="civilian-ships"><strong>Civilian ships and warships:</strong> Trireme, Sail and Transport are civilian ships: away from home they never make the citizens of a Republic or Democracy unhappy, and they upgrade into each other (Trireme → Sail → Transport). Frigate, Ironclad, Cruiser and Battleship are warships: they cause unhappiness and upgrade along their own line (Frigate → Ironclad → Cruiser → Battleship). The Frigate stays a mixed unit — it fights and still carries two land units — but now costs 50 shields instead of 40.</li>
    <li id="civilopedia-entries"><strong>New Civilopedia entries and Alt+H notes:</strong> <strong>Civilopedia → Miscellaneous</strong> has three new multi-page entries — <em>Diplomacy</em>, <em>War</em> and <em>Automation</em> — that explain the whole algorithm of the computer's diplomacy, the stack rule, the production queue, automated settlers and GoTo. <code>Alt+H</code> now ends with two notes listing the remastered keys and these entries.</li>
    <li id="extras-file"><strong>Compatible saves:</strong> Production queues and settler automation are saved in a small file next to the game (<code>CIVIL0.EXT</code>, like <code>CIVIL0.HP</code> for hit points), so the original DOS game can still load the save.</li>
    <li id="michelangelo"><strong>Strengthened Michelangelo's Chapel:</strong> Increases the effectiveness of all Cathedrals by 50% (6 content citizens instead of 4).</li>
    <li id="boarding"><strong>End turn on boarding:</strong> Boarding a ship ends the unit's moves for the current turn, preventing invasion exploits.</li>
    <li id="missing-files"><strong>Missing game files assistant:</strong> Helpful dialog with troubleshooting guidance if files are missing from the <code>CIVILIZATION</code> directory.</li>
    <li><strong>Palace view rendering:</strong> Architectural palace components render without misplaced pixels.</li>
    <li><strong>Interface and mouse stability:</strong> The game does not freeze on edge mouse clicks or when ending turns.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyboard navigation:</strong> Arrow keys work reliably across all menus and dialog popups.</li>
    <li><strong>VGA aspect ratio and palette:</strong> Accurate 4:3 pixel aspect ratio and authentic VGA color palette in windowed mode.</li>
    <li><strong>Dissolve transitions:</strong> Title logo, newly constructed improvements, palace view, and end-game replays appear with smooth dissolve effects.</li>
    <li><strong>Maximized window & fullscreen:</strong> Launches in a maximized window by default; F11 toggles borderless fullscreen.</li>
    <li><strong>Optimized download size:</strong> Standalone cross-platform binaries take only ~20 MB instead of 46 MB.</li>
  </ul>

